.Dd August 13, 2026
.Dt VIRTIO_SND 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm virtio_snd
.Nd VirtIO sound driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile this driver into the kernel,
place the following lines in your
kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "device sound"
.Cd "device virtio_snd"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternatively, to load the driver as a
module at boot time, place the following line in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
virtio_snd_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
device driver provides support for VirtIO sound devices,
bridging the device to the
.Xr pcm 4
sound framework.
.Pp
The driver exports a single playback (transmit) stream and a single
capture (receive) stream.
Both streams operate at signed 16-bit little-endian samples, two channels,
at the sample rates the device advertises (44.1 and 48 kHz).
Each attached device registers a
.Xr pcm 4
sound card, accessible through the usual
.Pa /dev/dsp Ns Ar N
interfaces.
The device has no hardware mixer; a stub software mixer is presented so that
the standard
.Xr mixer 8
controls are available.
.Pp
The driver speaks the VirtIO sound wire protocol directly.
Control-queue commands are issued synchronously, and the transmit and
capture data paths stage one period at a time through a coherent bounce
buffer, keeping a single transfer in flight per stream.
Both split and packed virtqueues are supported transparently by the shared
VirtIO transport layer.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pcm 4 ,
.Xr sound 4 ,
.Xr virtio 4 ,
.Xr mixer 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 15.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
driver was written by
.An Kory Heard .
.Sh BUGS
This is a first-pass implementation.
Only the fixed stereo S16_LE format at 44.1 and 48 kHz is supported, a
single stream is exported in each direction, and the
.Dq controls
feature is neither offered nor used.
